# Rank 1819 / CVE-2026-28815 (CWE-125): apple/swift-crypto's BoringSSL-backed
# X-Wing private key decapsulates an attacker-supplied HPKE ciphertext without
# first checking its length. OpenSSLXWingPrivateKeyImpl.Backing.decapsulate(_:)
# (Sources/Crypto/KEM/BoringSSL/XWing_boring.swift at fix parent fa308c0) takes
# `encapsulated: Data` straight from the KEM/HPKE entry points, opens it with
# `withUnsafeBytes`, and passes `encapsulatedSecretBytes.baseAddress` as the
# `ciphertext` parameter of CCryptoBoringSSL_XWING_decap -- a C function whose
# header (Sources/CCryptoBoringSSL/include/CCryptoBoringSSL_xwing.h:91)
# declares `const uint8_t ciphertext[XWING_CIPHERTEXT_BYTES]` (1120) with no
# length parameter, so the callee reads 1120 bytes regardless of what the
# sender sent. A truncated encapsulated key reads past the buffer. Fix
# bb4ba815 adds `guard encapsulated.count == Int(XWING_CIPHERTEXT_BYTES) else
# { throw CryptoKitError.incorrectParameterSize }` before the call, exactly
# the guard every sibling in the same package carries: the MLKEM768
# and MLKEM1024 decapsulate(_:) implementations open with
# `guard encapsulated.count == <ciphertextByteCount> else { throw
# CryptoKitError.incorrectParameterSize }` (MLKEM_boring.swift:144/411), and
# XWing_boring.swift's own key imports guard `$0.count == XWING_PUBLIC_KEY_BYTES`
# and `ptr.count == Int(XWING_PRIVATE_KEY_BYTES) + Int(XWING_PUBLIC_KEY_BYTES)`
# before touching baseAddress. The pool row is correct: the vulnerable shape
# is present at the parent, the remediation is present at the fixed revision, and the
# merge commit's combined diff reads in full.
#
# ATTENTION, two stacked reasons, both measured.
#
# First, an extraction gap, now fixed in the companion commit: before that
# fix, the entire decapsulate body contributed no nodes at all. `vyql graph`
# on XWing_boring.swift at the parent showed no call node for
# `encapsulated.withUnsafeBytes` and none for CCryptoBoringSSL_XWING_decap,
# while the same calls without the `try` prefix extracted normally --
# because both statements are `try_expression` nodes in statement position,
# a kind (*swConv).stmt's switch had no case for, so the whole statement
# (trailing-closure bodies included) drops out. expr() treats
# try_expression as transparent (nir.Thru, swift.go:670); stmt() now lowers
# the payload the same way, so `try S` and `S` lower alike and the second
# case below reports. Measured effect on findings: none -- union over three
# `--cache off` runs per tree on seven Swift trees (swift-crypto vulnerable
# and fixed, swift-asn1, swift-nio, swift-corelibs-foundation, sparkle,
# starscream) is byte-identical before and after (1291/1291, 0, 252, 30,
# 330, 0 findings), because no shipped Swift binding names an API called
# from a `try` statement in any of them. The change is pure recall enablement:
# the decap call now extracts, verified at the graph level.
#
# Second, with the calls extracted, no binding can discriminate the guard,
# so no detection ships. A bare `callExpr where callee.path ==
# "CCryptoBoringSSL_XWING_decap"` binding (probed against the live
# VYQL-DOS-040 concept) fires 1 on the vulnerable tree and 0 on the fixed
# tree -- but only as an artifact of the changeing commit's own layout: the
# fix also changed `try SymmetricKey(...)` to `return try SymmetricKey(...)`,
# and a return-wrapped trailing closure still drops its body (the
# control_transfer_statement path runs expr() without trailingLambdaStmts),
# so the decap call is invisible at the fixed revision for a reason
# unrelated to the guard. Proven by the alternate spelling: keeping the
# guard and only dropping the `return` makes the same binding fire on
# correctly-patched code. Keying on that is the rejected
# fixing-commit's-own-layout discriminator. The honest routes are all
# measured dead: the file-wide module context carries
# identifier:XWING_CIPHERTEXT_BYTES at BOTH revisions (the encap path
# allocates with it), so a module-scope negation suppresses both; and
# node.context.* predicates are rejected on callExpr bindings for Swift
# (the 1792 finding), while presenceNode's callPath route is AST-routed and
# inherits the same return-drop artifact. What the weakness needs is
# "encapsulated.count compares to the ciphertext size in this function,
# at the call immediately above" -- the filed combination of Swift
# having no per-function context facts (rank 1792) and stmt's guard/if
# condition handling keeping only the first condition (rank 1217), plus a
# buffer-count-to-call correlation no frontend offers (the C analogue filed
# at rank 1676). Named, not re-filed.
#
# Considered and declined: binding the decap call's ciphertext argument as
# a review sink with no negation reports the patched revision too (the call
# remains, correctly, at the fixed revision) -- a label that fires on fixed code
# with no discriminator is the shape the review queue rejects, and on this
# two-file tree it is 1 finding at each revision.
#
# Known recall boundaries, stated rather than hidden: `return try X { ... }`
# still drops the trailing-closure body (the fixed revision's own spelling),
# and `try await X { ... }` lowers the await_expression payload to nothing
# -- both pre-existing, unchanged by the companion commit, which fixes only
# the bare `try expr` statement.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-engine-gap; cases 1 and 2 (the shipped
# VYQL-RF-001 try-prefix isolation pair) expected, cases 3 and 4 pinned.

test "rank1819: engine gap isolated -- environment-sourced URL fetched inside a trailing closure reports"
  lang swift
  file XWingProbe.swift
  expect VYQL-RF-001
  code
  ```
  import Foundation

  func handler(encapsulated: Data) {
      let u = ProcessInfo.processInfo.environment["URL"]!
      encapsulated.withUnsafeBytes { raw in
          URL(string: u)
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1819: engine gap isolated -- the identical call in the identical closure, prefixed with try, reports the same"
  lang swift
  file XWingProbeTry.swift
  expect VYQL-RF-001
  code
  ```
  import Foundation

  func handler(encapsulated: Data) throws {
      let u = ProcessInfo.processInfo.environment["URL"]!
      try encapsulated.withUnsafeBytes { raw in
          URL(string: u)
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1819: X-Wing decapsulation hands the encapsulated key's baseAddress to the fixed-size native decap with no length check"
  lang swift
  file XWing_boring.swift
  reject VYQL-MEM-064
  code
  ```
  import Foundation

  enum CryptoKitError: Error {
      case incorrectParameterSize
      case internalBoringSSLError()
  }

  struct SymmetricKey {
      init(unsafeUninitializedCapacity: Int, _ body: (inout UnsafeMutableRawBufferPointer, inout Int) throws -> Void) throws {}
  }

  struct XWING_private_key {}

  func CCryptoBoringSSL_XWING_decap(_ out: UnsafeMutableRawPointer, _ ciphertext: UnsafeRawPointer, _ key: UnsafeMutablePointer<XWING_private_key>) -> CInt { 1 }

  struct OpenSSLXWingPrivateKeyImpl {
      var privateKey: XWING_private_key

      func decapsulate(_ encapsulated: Data) throws -> SymmetricKey {
          try SymmetricKey(unsafeUninitializedCapacity: 32) { sharedSecretBytes, count in
              try encapsulated.withUnsafeBytes { encapsulatedSecretBytes in
                  let rc = CCryptoBoringSSL_XWING_decap(
                      sharedSecretBytes.baseAddress,
                      encapsulatedSecretBytes.baseAddress,
                      &self.privateKey
                  )
                  guard rc == 1 else {
                      throw CryptoKitError.internalBoringSSLError()
                  }
                  count = 32
              }
          }
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1819: the patched decapsulation guards the ciphertext length before opening the buffer"
  lang swift
  file XWing_boring_fixed.swift
  reject VYQL-MEM-064
  code
  ```
  import Foundation

  enum CryptoKitError: Error {
      case incorrectParameterSize
      case internalBoringSSLError()
  }

  struct SymmetricKey {
      init(unsafeUninitializedCapacity: Int, _ body: (inout UnsafeMutableRawBufferPointer, inout Int) throws -> Void) throws {}
  }

  struct XWING_private_key {}

  func CCryptoBoringSSL_XWING_decap(_ out: UnsafeMutableRawPointer, _ ciphertext: UnsafeRawPointer, _ key: UnsafeMutablePointer<XWING_private_key>) -> CInt { 1 }

  let XWING_CIPHERTEXT_BYTES = 1120

  struct OpenSSLXWingPrivateKeyImpl {
      var privateKey: XWING_private_key

      func decapsulate(_ encapsulated: Data) throws -> SymmetricKey {
          guard encapsulated.count == Int(XWING_CIPHERTEXT_BYTES) else {
              throw CryptoKitError.incorrectParameterSize
          }

          return try SymmetricKey(unsafeUninitializedCapacity: 32) { sharedSecretBytes, count in
              try encapsulated.withUnsafeBytes { encapsulatedSecretBytes in
                  let rc = CCryptoBoringSSL_XWING_decap(
                      sharedSecretBytes.baseAddress,
                      encapsulatedSecretBytes.baseAddress,
                      &self.privateKey
                  )
                  guard rc == 1 else {
                      throw CryptoKitError.internalBoringSSLError()
                  }
                  count = 32
              }
          }
      }
  }
  ```
